1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,000
More and more of everything we make

2
00:00:03,100 --> 00:00:05,600
is the product of a machine.

3
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:08,400
But a special class of products can be created only

4
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:11,340
by this wonder of engineering, the human hand.

5
00:00:15,440 --> 00:00:18,760
In a world that values robotic precision and efficiency,

6
00:00:18,860 --> 00:00:24,560
we still revere a keen eye, nimble fingers,

7
00:00:24,660 --> 00:00:26,260
and a craftsman's intellect.

8
00:00:26,360 --> 00:00:28,760
Every aspect of this company is hands on.

9
00:00:28,761 --> 00:00:30,960
If you demand something truly unique,

10
00:00:31,060 --> 00:00:34,460
you've got to go old school.

11
00:00:34,560 --> 00:00:35,900
I'm a tailor for people.

12
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:37,000
I just work in metal.

13
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:40,700
So roll up your sleeves and limber up.

14
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:43,900
Now, Built by Hand on Modern Marvels.

15
00:00:58,760 --> 00:01:01,320
We've spent billions trying to replicate it,

16
00:01:01,321 --> 00:01:05,540
and with each passing decade, we edge a little closer.

17
00:01:05,541 --> 00:01:07,700
But no device yet invented matches

18
00:01:07,701 --> 00:01:10,980
the precision and versatility of the amazing tool that's

19
00:01:10,981 --> 00:01:14,800
just an arm's length away.

20
00:01:14,801 --> 00:01:17,720
The human hand is a marvel of bioengineering,

21
00:01:17,721 --> 00:01:21,380
typically made up of 27 bones, hundreds of ligaments, tendons,

22
00:01:21,381 --> 00:01:24,560
and muscles, and millions of nerves.

23
00:01:24,561 --> 00:01:27,760
No other appendage in the animal kingdom is like it.

24
00:01:27,761 --> 00:01:30,100
We all know that humans as well as primates

25
00:01:30,101 --> 00:01:32,840
have an opposable thumb. But what makes us unique

26
00:01:32,841 --> 00:01:34,840
is the fact that we have a precision grip,

27
00:01:34,841 --> 00:01:37,260
the ability to touch our thumb to our pinky

28
00:01:37,261 --> 00:01:40,280
as well as our ring fingers. And that's what makes

29
00:01:40,281 --> 00:01:44,780
the human hand such an incredible tool.

30
00:01:44,781 --> 00:01:47,580
And this incredibly dexterous tool still reigns

31
00:01:47,581 --> 00:01:50,440
as the most effective means to craft some products

32
00:01:50,441 --> 00:01:57,760
that demand a finesse that no machine can manage.

33
00:01:57,761 --> 00:01:59,420
Some are even willing to bet their lives

34
00:01:59,520 --> 00:02:05,640
on the handiwork of others, like New Mexico glider

35
00:02:05,740 --> 00:02:09,140
pilot Bob Carlton.

36
00:02:09,240 --> 00:02:11,240
You know, this plane is hand-built.

37
00:02:11,340 --> 00:02:15,140
So every time I climb in, I'm sort of putting my life

38
00:02:15,240 --> 00:02:17,940
in the hands of the craftsman that built it.

39
00:02:18,040 --> 00:02:19,880
Emergency parachute.

40
00:02:19,980 --> 00:02:22,280
Bob himself handcrafted some modifications

41
00:02:22,380 --> 00:02:25,920
to this glider, tapping his experience as a machinist

42
00:02:25,921 --> 00:02:27,660
and a rocket scientist.

43
00:02:27,760 --> 00:02:29,820
The coolest modification of all?

44
00:02:29,920 --> 00:02:31,920
A little something to help him get airborne

45
00:02:32,020 --> 00:02:34,060
without an assist from a tow plane.

46
00:02:34,160 --> 00:02:37,460
This glider has something rather unique.

47
00:02:37,560 --> 00:02:39,160
It has its own jet engine.

48
00:02:39,260 --> 00:02:44,380
This engine is the TJ-100, built in the Czech Republic.

49
00:02:44,480 --> 00:02:46,480
It makes about 250 pounds of thrust,

50
00:02:46,580 --> 00:02:47,880
and it's enough to get this thing in the air

51
00:02:47,980 --> 00:02:51,080
without any problems at all.

52
00:02:51,180 --> 00:02:55,920
Bob is a star in the international air show circuit.

53
00:02:55,921 --> 00:02:58,820
And each glider he owns is designed to be broken down

54
00:02:58,920 --> 00:03:01,820
and later rebuilt to make transportation easier.

55
00:03:04,220 --> 00:03:06,260
We do air shows all over the world,

56
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:10,700
and every place we go, we have to disassemble the plane

57
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:12,540
and reassemble it again.

58
00:03:12,640 --> 00:03:13,840
OK, now we got it.

59
00:03:16,700 --> 00:03:18,440
Assembling an aircraft by hand

60
00:03:18,540 --> 00:03:21,120
leaves no margin for error.

61
00:03:21,220 --> 00:03:25,920
Bob's peace of mind comes from doing the job himself.

62
00:03:25,921 --> 00:03:30,000
It's just nice knowing every time I fly it that I'm the one

63
00:03:30,100 --> 00:03:32,060
that put it together.

64
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:34,660
Everything's put together right.

65
00:03:34,760 --> 00:03:35,660
Here we go.

66
00:03:42,600 --> 00:03:44,940
As Bob gains speed and altitude,

67
00:03:45,040 --> 00:03:48,240
aerodynamic stresses grow on the hand-built wings, tail,

68
00:03:48,340 --> 00:03:50,740
rudder, and fuselage of his fiberglass aircraft.

69
00:03:50,741 --> 00:03:57,740
We'll just open the doors and retract the engine,

70
00:03:57,741 --> 00:04:01,420
and close the doors again, and we are a glider.

71
00:04:01,421 --> 00:04:03,680
And man, what a nice day to fly.

72
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:08,760
Every flight Bob takes links him

73
00:04:08,761 --> 00:04:13,860
to aviation's pioneering hand craftsman, the Wright Brothers.

74
00:04:13,861 --> 00:04:17,040
They flew a plane featuring hand-carved spruce propellers,

75
00:04:17,041 --> 00:04:20,740
a hand-built engine with no carburetor or spark plugs.

76
00:04:20,741 --> 00:04:25,740
and wings covered with fabric sewn together by their sister.

77
00:04:25,741 --> 00:04:29,240
When the Wright brothers first flew their airplane in 1903,

78
00:04:29,241 --> 00:04:33,240
the flight was only about 12 seconds long, very low, slow

79
00:04:33,241 --> 00:04:36,240
flight, and this is a natural extension of what the Wright

80
00:04:36,241 --> 00:04:39,740
brothers started in the early 1900s.

81
00:04:39,741 --> 00:04:42,740
Handcrafted aircraft have come a long way since the Wright

82
00:04:42,741 --> 00:04:45,740
brothers, thanks to materials like fiberglass, a better

83
00:04:45,741 --> 00:04:49,740
understanding of aerodynamics, not to mention a jet engine.

84
00:04:49,741 --> 00:04:56,740
engine.

85
00:04:56,840 --> 00:05:03,680
For Bob Carlton, a glider built by hand is the only way to fly.

86
00:05:03,780 --> 00:05:04,880
Wow, great flight.

87
00:05:13,800 --> 00:05:16,940
And for the Morley family of Swan Lake, Montana,

88
00:05:16,941 --> 00:05:19,840
No machine-made canoe glides across the water like the

89
00:05:19,940 --> 00:05:23,840
handcrafted ones that carry the Morley name.

90
00:05:23,940 --> 00:05:26,040
Well, what I love about a wooden boat is the way it

91
00:05:26,140 --> 00:05:27,380
handles in the water.

92
00:05:27,480 --> 00:05:30,680
It seems to take on a life of its own, probably because it's

93
00:05:30,780 --> 00:05:34,600
been carved out of something that's been living, unlike

94
00:05:34,700 --> 00:05:38,500
plastic or metal boats.

95
00:05:38,600 --> 00:05:40,300
Greg Morley has been handcrafting

96
00:05:40,400 --> 00:05:43,200
wooden canoes since 1971.

97
00:05:43,300 --> 00:05:46,500
His old school technique is found an eager clientele that

98
00:05:46,501 --> 00:05:48,840
that prizes his handiwork because it's anything but

99
00:05:48,841 --> 00:05:52,100
off the rack. We're ready to build a 17-foot

100
00:05:52,101 --> 00:05:55,840
guide model canoe. First step is to take the forms

101
00:05:55,841 --> 00:06:01,340
that are already prepared for it and put them on the strongback.

102
00:06:01,341 --> 00:06:03,820
Once all the forms are secured on the strongback,

103
00:06:03,821 --> 00:06:06,460
it takes two days of precision and patience to construct the

104
00:06:06,461 --> 00:06:10,300
hull piece by piece. The first part that is attached

105
00:06:10,301 --> 00:06:14,200
to the forms are the stems that define the bow and the stern

106
00:06:14,201 --> 00:06:19,383
ends of the boat. We've pre-selected this cedar from our stock of cedar here.

107
00:06:19,384 --> 00:06:20,460
We selected this

108
00:06:20,461 --> 00:06:21,648
for a nice dark color.

109
00:06:21,649 --> 00:06:25,480
And these strips will be glued edge to edge with a bead and cove joint.

110
00:06:26,300 --> 00:06:30,843
Allows them to conform to the form, bending around complex turns.

111
00:06:30,844 --> 00:06:32,660
And unlike ancient canoes,

112
00:06:32,840 --> 00:06:38,140
which were typically carved out of one log, we use about 125 pieces of wood to make this boat.

113
00:06:38,141 --> 00:06:42,780
After the canoe's basic shape is completed, Greg and Steve

114
00:06:42,880 --> 00:06:46,740
handcraft all the necessary accessories, including the seats

115
00:06:46,840 --> 00:06:49,680
with traditional rawhide webbing.

116
00:06:49,780 --> 00:06:55,500
This method was used 200 years ago by the French Canadians.

117
00:06:55,600 --> 00:06:59,800
When it dries on the frame, it'll shrink up drum tight.

118
00:07:02,800 --> 00:07:05,440
The Morleys customize the handcrafted paddles

119
00:07:05,441 --> 00:07:07,940
paddles according to their intended use.

120
00:07:07,941 --> 00:07:09,140
It's going to be whitewater.

121
00:07:09,141 --> 00:07:11,140
We can make it a little thicker through the blade.

122
00:07:11,141 --> 00:07:13,940
We can adjust the size of the grip so that they can fit

123
00:07:13,941 --> 00:07:19,280
comfortably with someone if they have larger hands.

124
00:07:19,281 --> 00:07:21,980
On request, the Morley's will add an artistic touch

125
00:07:21,981 --> 00:07:27,100
that no automated paint sprayer can duplicate.

126
00:07:27,101 --> 00:07:30,040
Greg and Steve also customize their boats with wood inlay

127
00:07:30,041 --> 00:07:34,140
designs.

128
00:07:34,141 --> 00:07:36,540
We do much of our work here by hand,

129
00:07:36,640 --> 00:07:39,640
and some with various power tools.

130
00:07:39,740 --> 00:07:43,380
But we do a little bit with this 1870 scroll saw, too.

131
00:07:43,480 --> 00:07:46,680
The inlay work that we do is done on this machine.

132
00:07:52,200 --> 00:07:56,000
The impressive final products go for around $4,500

133
00:07:56,100 --> 00:07:59,540
and are sometimes purchased not for recreation, but as art.

134
00:07:59,640 --> 00:08:01,500
They're as satisfying to the makers

135
00:08:01,501 --> 00:08:03,500
are to the customers that buy them.

136
00:08:03,600 --> 00:08:06,500
When you spend a month working on a boat,

137
00:08:06,600 --> 00:08:08,500
it's nice to know who the customer is,

138
00:08:08,600 --> 00:08:10,500
and you can think about how they're

139
00:08:10,600 --> 00:08:13,040
going to use this boat over the next several years,

140
00:08:13,140 --> 00:08:15,940
and what kind of enjoyment this boat's going to bring them.

141
00:08:24,160 --> 00:08:27,500
If you think a $4,500 canoe is pricey,

142
00:08:27,600 --> 00:08:30,500
how about a $12,000 knife?

143
00:08:30,501 --> 00:08:33,340
That's what one of Bob Kramer's blades fetched at an online

144
00:08:33,440 --> 00:08:35,500
auction in 2010.

145
00:08:35,600 --> 00:08:39,200
Bob, owner of Kramer Knives, licenses some of his kitchen

146
00:08:39,300 --> 00:08:42,440
knife designs to the retail market.

147
00:08:42,540 --> 00:08:45,120
But it's in his Olympia, Washington workshop that he

148
00:08:45,220 --> 00:08:49,960
handcrafts about 200 specialty knives annually.

149
00:08:50,060 --> 00:08:52,560
Bob showed us a condensed version of the painstaking

150
00:08:52,660 --> 00:08:56,060
process that usually takes him four days to complete.

151
00:08:56,160 --> 00:08:58,660
I start with three different steels.

152
00:08:58,661 --> 00:09:01,700
Each one has a slightly different chemistry to it.

153
00:09:01,800 --> 00:09:05,640
And by combining these three steels and forge welding them

154
00:09:05,740 --> 00:09:09,700
together, I get a unique aesthetic and cutting ability

155
00:09:09,800 --> 00:09:11,400
in my knives.

156
00:09:11,500 --> 00:09:14,000
Bob tacked welds the pieces of steel

157
00:09:14,100 --> 00:09:18,240
into what's called a billet and adds a temporary handle.

158
00:09:18,340 --> 00:09:20,820
I've got my billet at 25 pieces of steel,

159
00:09:20,920 --> 00:09:23,720
and I'm going to pop them into the forge, which is running at

160
00:09:23,820 --> 00:09:26,520
2,350 degrees Fahrenheit.

161
00:09:26,521 --> 00:09:30,020
Initially, the layers are close together,

162
00:09:30,021 --> 00:09:32,020
but not that close together.

163
00:09:34,020 --> 00:09:37,020
So we get them hot enough so that they become plastic.

164
00:09:42,020 --> 00:09:44,020
The first time underneath the hammer,

165
00:09:44,021 --> 00:09:46,020
we're actually pushing the layers

166
00:09:46,021 --> 00:09:49,020
so that they're in close enough contact

167
00:09:49,021 --> 00:09:51,520
so that when they go back into the furnace,

168
00:09:51,521 --> 00:09:54,020
there is actually a molecular exchange

169
00:09:54,021 --> 00:09:56,520
across that interface on the layers.

170
00:10:01,620 --> 00:10:04,160
Subsequent trips into the forge and under the hammer

171
00:10:04,260 --> 00:10:05,860
make the billet wider and longer.

172
00:10:10,460 --> 00:10:16,080
Next, Bob cuts it into five pieces, stacks them,

173
00:10:16,180 --> 00:10:18,980
and then re-welds them into a new billet.

174
00:10:19,080 --> 00:10:21,080
I'll put it back into the forge,

175
00:10:21,180 --> 00:10:22,780
where I'll weld those up together,

176
00:10:22,781 --> 00:10:24,980
so we'll have 125 layers.

177
00:10:25,080 --> 00:10:27,320
I'll do this process a number of times

178
00:10:27,420 --> 00:10:31,180
until I get to 1,000 layers.

179
00:10:31,280 --> 00:10:34,220
Every layer Bob adds increases the metal's strength.

180
00:10:34,320 --> 00:10:37,060
Once he's up to 1,000, he sends the final billet

181
00:10:37,160 --> 00:10:37,960
through a roller mill.

182
00:10:45,540 --> 00:10:49,140
So now I've got a billet that's 1,000 layers

183
00:10:49,240 --> 00:10:52,780
and a consistent thickness and a consistent width.

184
00:10:52,781 --> 00:10:56,820
Next, a ladder mill's imprint will result in the unique

185
00:10:56,920 --> 00:11:00,020
undulating patterns found in each of Bob's finished blades.

186
00:11:00,120 --> 00:11:04,020
So now it's consistently laddered from top to bottom,

187
00:11:04,120 --> 00:11:07,520
and we'll let this cool off and then grind off the high spots

188
00:11:07,620 --> 00:11:10,940
and just have that center section.

189
00:11:11,040 --> 00:11:13,340
After cutting out the shape of the blade,

190
00:11:13,440 --> 00:11:16,140
Bob pounds out the knife's edge on an anvil.

191
00:11:16,240 --> 00:11:20,440
I'm hand-forging this blade the same way

192
00:11:20,441 --> 00:11:24,380
that it's been forged for thousands of years.

193
00:11:28,380 --> 00:11:30,380
After a little grinding, it's time

194
00:11:30,480 --> 00:11:32,780
for another time-honored bladesmithing technique

195
00:11:32,880 --> 00:11:35,280
that hardens the knife's steel.

196
00:11:35,380 --> 00:11:37,200
I'm in the heat treatment process,

197
00:11:37,300 --> 00:11:40,200
where I'm bringing the steel up to a particular temperature,

198
00:11:40,300 --> 00:11:43,300
and then I'm going to quench it in this oil bath to harden it.

199
00:11:47,800 --> 00:11:50,440
Now, the steel is getting as hard as it gets.

200
00:11:50,441 --> 00:11:52,140
can get.

201
00:11:52,141 --> 00:11:54,140
This step is crucial.

202
00:11:54,141 --> 00:11:58,340
It is the heart and soul of this knife.

203
00:11:58,341 --> 00:12:00,940
A three-hour bath and an etching solution adds yet

204
00:12:00,941 --> 00:12:03,580
another dimension to the knife.

205
00:12:03,581 --> 00:12:05,900
The three types of steel react to the caustic liquid

206
00:12:05,901 --> 00:12:11,140
at different rates, creating unique patterns in each blade.

207
00:12:11,141 --> 00:12:12,980
So there's the finished etch.

208
00:12:12,981 --> 00:12:16,600
And you can feel the pattern as well as see it.

209
00:12:16,601 --> 00:12:19,140
I never get tired of looking at these.

210
00:12:20,440 --> 00:12:24,440
Every finished knife brings Bob a lot of satisfaction,

211
00:12:24,441 --> 00:12:28,440
almost as much as showing us how sharp and durable they are.

212
00:12:28,441 --> 00:12:31,440
This is the first test of the American Bladesmith Society

213
00:12:31,441 --> 00:12:33,440
Master Smith exam.

214
00:12:33,441 --> 00:12:36,440
This is a one-inch sisal rope.

215
00:12:36,441 --> 00:12:39,440
I have to cut it no more than six inches from the end,

216
00:12:39,441 --> 00:12:42,440
and it's just free-swinging in the air there.

217
00:12:42,441 --> 00:12:49,440
This thing will be around longer than both of us.

218
00:12:49,540 --> 00:12:50,580
And I love that.

219
00:12:50,680 --> 00:12:52,080
And it's a tool.

220
00:12:52,180 --> 00:12:57,180
It's not just meant to sit in a case and make dinner with this.

221
00:13:12,440 --> 00:13:17,440
Most of the weapons of war wielded around the globe today

222
00:13:17,540 --> 00:13:19,440
have one thing in common.

223
00:13:19,540 --> 00:13:22,440
They're the result of industrial production lines.

224
00:13:22,540 --> 00:13:24,440
But for most of man's history, weaponry

225
00:13:24,540 --> 00:13:27,440
was crafted exclusively by hand.

226
00:13:27,540 --> 00:13:30,440
The suits of armor you see here are from the European

227
00:13:30,540 --> 00:13:33,440
continent in the Great Isle of Britain and Scotland

228
00:13:33,540 --> 00:13:35,440
from the 15th century.

229
00:13:35,540 --> 00:13:38,440
These armor suits are an exoskeleton of the human body.

230
00:13:38,540 --> 00:13:41,440
It is functional 14-gauge armor ready for the world.

231
00:13:41,441 --> 00:13:44,940
ready for battle. Metalsmith Bart Remling

232
00:13:44,941 --> 00:13:47,940
handcrafted all this historically accurate armor.

233
00:13:55,380 --> 00:13:57,860
Bart makes his suits of armor in this old barn,

234
00:13:57,861 --> 00:14:01,200
in the farmland of central California.

235
00:14:01,201 --> 00:14:04,300
And like the armor of yore, each suit is custom-built

236
00:14:04,301 --> 00:14:07,300
to perfectly fit the client. I'm a tailor for people.

237
00:14:07,301 --> 00:14:11,440
I just work in metal. That's my medium, my material.

238
00:14:11,441 --> 00:14:15,240
This is a 15th century German Gothic blued steel.

239
00:14:15,241 --> 00:14:19,340
There's over 375 pieces in the armor.

240
00:14:19,341 --> 00:14:23,200
Every piece is made by hand, intricately done.

241
00:14:23,201 --> 00:14:24,940
And this is my suit personally.

242
00:14:28,360 --> 00:14:31,940
One of BART's suits will set you back about $4,500,

243
00:14:31,941 --> 00:14:34,960
or more depending on the amount of detail desired.

244
00:14:34,961 --> 00:14:36,280
But they're a relative bargain when

245
00:14:36,281 --> 00:14:38,780
compared to the medieval originals.

246
00:14:38,781 --> 00:14:41,100
Back in the old days, it would have been the equivalent

247
00:14:41,101 --> 00:14:43,600
a modern Maserati for the knights of old.

248
00:14:43,700 --> 00:14:46,600
So it was quite cost prohibitive.

249
00:14:46,700 --> 00:14:49,000
Bart gets a jump start on the medieval armor making

250
00:14:49,100 --> 00:14:53,340
process by starting with 14 gauge sheets of steel.

251
00:14:53,440 --> 00:14:55,640
I'm going to build a shoulder assembly today

252
00:14:55,740 --> 00:14:57,040
known as a spaulder.

253
00:14:57,140 --> 00:14:59,660
I have my steel clamped to the table.

254
00:14:59,760 --> 00:15:03,060
We have our own patterns that we design ourselves.

255
00:15:03,160 --> 00:15:07,260
We trace around the pattern, and now it's ready to be cut out.

256
00:15:07,360 --> 00:15:09,660
The old armorsmiths used a shear.

257
00:15:09,661 --> 00:15:11,160
We used the modern jigsaw.

258
00:15:16,600 --> 00:15:22,100
Next, Bart goes medieval with his technique,

259
00:15:22,200 --> 00:15:26,000
with the help of a variety of anvils.

260
00:15:26,100 --> 00:15:30,720
He works solo, taking at least a month to complete one suit.

261
00:15:30,820 --> 00:15:33,920
The medieval pace was far quicker.

262
00:15:34,020 --> 00:15:37,160
There were hundreds of guys producing a suit of armor.

263
00:15:37,161 --> 00:15:42,660
That's how they could manage to make 1,000 suits of armor.

264
00:15:42,760 --> 00:15:44,360
Shaping the armor marks the steel

265
00:15:44,460 --> 00:15:46,600
with coin-like impressions.

266
00:15:46,700 --> 00:15:49,500
The challenge now is to smooth the surface.

267
00:15:49,600 --> 00:15:53,920
Nowadays, I have a little trick that I use called a planishing

268
00:15:54,020 --> 00:15:54,720
hammer.

269
00:15:57,620 --> 00:15:59,120
And that's how I'll go ahead and do it.

270
00:15:59,220 --> 00:16:00,420
It speeds up the process.

271
00:16:03,160 --> 00:16:06,520
What used to take me two days, now it takes me about two

272
00:16:06,521 --> 00:16:13,160
minutes. After Bart shapes each piece,

273
00:16:13,260 --> 00:16:22,160
he polishes it. At this point, Bart's medieval

274
00:16:22,260 --> 00:16:25,780
ancestors would have heated the molded pieces in a forge to

275
00:16:25,880 --> 00:16:29,780
temper or strengthen the steel. Bart's blowtorch achieves the

276
00:16:29,880 --> 00:16:35,020
same result, heating it to about 525 degrees Fahrenheit. The

277
00:16:35,021 --> 00:16:38,020
The intense temperature also turns the steel blue, which

278
00:16:38,120 --> 00:16:41,260
is historically accurate.

279
00:16:41,360 --> 00:16:45,560
Blue armor was battle-ready armor.

280
00:16:45,660 --> 00:16:48,460
The shiny silver armor you often see in movies also

281
00:16:48,560 --> 00:16:51,360
existed in medieval times, but was primarily used just

282
00:16:51,460 --> 00:16:54,980
for ceremonial events.

283
00:16:55,080 --> 00:16:57,880
Once Bart finishes making the hundreds of individual pieces

284
00:16:57,980 --> 00:17:00,380
needed for one suit, his next challenge

285
00:17:00,480 --> 00:17:02,080
is to carefully assemble them.

286
00:17:02,180 --> 00:17:04,380
So the piece of armor you saw me making

287
00:17:04,381 --> 00:17:06,380
goes to this shoulder assembly.

288
00:17:06,480 --> 00:17:09,280
It's quite precise, quite fluid in movement.

289
00:17:12,720 --> 00:17:14,820
As you can see, it works quite well.

290
00:17:14,920 --> 00:17:16,660
A lot of people don't realize this,

291
00:17:16,760 --> 00:17:20,000
how the armor is put together with leather.

292
00:17:20,100 --> 00:17:23,360
And that is so we can move fluidly and with ease.

293
00:17:25,900 --> 00:17:28,200
It isn't until Bart and his fellow reenactors

294
00:17:28,300 --> 00:17:31,000
suit up for battle that the precision and complexity

295
00:17:31,100 --> 00:17:33,940
of his handiwork can be fully appreciated.

296
00:17:33,941 --> 00:17:36,780
It also dispels a common misunderstanding.

297
00:17:36,880 --> 00:17:39,140
Although each can weigh up to 100 pounds,

298
00:17:39,240 --> 00:17:40,780
they still allow for fluid movement.

299
00:17:44,680 --> 00:17:46,720
The common belief about armor is that it's

300
00:17:46,820 --> 00:17:49,960
one large piece of steel that restricts the wearer's movement.

301
00:17:50,060 --> 00:17:51,620
This could not be further from the truth.

302
00:17:57,760 --> 00:18:00,400
For Bart and the others, the real fun lies

303
00:18:00,500 --> 00:18:01,900
in putting it to the ultimate test.

304
00:18:03,940 --> 00:18:05,440
He's a good one.

305
00:18:05,441 --> 00:18:10,940
Bart makes the swords as well.

306
00:18:10,941 --> 00:18:15,940
And unlike the action you might see at a Renaissance fair,

307
00:18:15,941 --> 00:18:18,940
these guys aren't holding back.

308
00:18:18,941 --> 00:18:23,940
It's a testament to the quality of Bart's work that these 21st

309
00:18:23,941 --> 00:18:27,940
century knights lived to fight another day.

310
00:18:27,941 --> 00:18:31,940
of these 21st century knights lived to fight another day.

311
00:18:31,941 --> 00:18:32,940
Ah!

312
00:18:32,941 --> 00:18:33,940
Huzzah!

313
00:18:43,940 --> 00:18:46,940
Connecticut gunsmith Edwin Perry is another craftsman

314
00:18:46,941 --> 00:18:49,940
who gets a bang out of making historically accurate weaponry

315
00:18:49,941 --> 00:18:50,940
by hand.

316
00:18:50,941 --> 00:18:53,940
All firearms were built by hand one at a time

317
00:18:53,941 --> 00:18:55,940
until about 1840.

318
00:18:55,941 --> 00:19:00,740
here, an early 1700s flintlock blunderbuss of Naval service.

319
00:19:00,840 --> 00:19:01,940
It's fired this way.

320
00:19:09,380 --> 00:19:11,180
A devastating close range weapon.

321
00:19:14,300 --> 00:19:18,400
Ed once served in the Navy as a submarine machinist,

322
00:19:18,500 --> 00:19:21,640
and now channels his skills into handcrafting 18th century

323
00:19:21,740 --> 00:19:25,040
firearms for gun collectors and reenactors.

324
00:19:25,041 --> 00:19:28,780
Today we're building Davy Crockett's first rifle.

325
00:19:28,880 --> 00:19:32,280
It's a Lancaster-style rifle, and we need to lay a pattern

326
00:19:32,380 --> 00:19:34,280
on here.

327
00:19:34,380 --> 00:19:35,680
Now that we've traced this on here,

328
00:19:35,780 --> 00:19:38,380
we're going to go to the bandsaw and finish the cuts.

329
00:19:44,900 --> 00:19:47,700
After this initial power tool assist,

330
00:19:47,800 --> 00:19:50,940
Ed crafts his firearms just as gunsmiths did hundreds

331
00:19:51,040 --> 00:19:53,440
of years ago.

332
00:19:53,441 --> 00:19:55,140
Now we're going to use an 18th century tool

333
00:19:55,240 --> 00:19:56,280
called a spokeshave.

334
00:19:56,380 --> 00:19:58,280
We're using the tools of the period

335
00:19:58,380 --> 00:20:00,680
to produce weapons of the period because they work.

336
00:20:03,980 --> 00:20:07,280
Figured hardwoods require you to go in different directions

337
00:20:07,380 --> 00:20:08,680
in different parts of the wood.

338
00:20:10,900 --> 00:20:13,400
Routers help Ed gouge out grooves for the rifle's

339
00:20:13,500 --> 00:20:16,500
handcrafted ramrod and barrel.

340
00:20:16,600 --> 00:20:18,300
Now after a lot of work, we've established

341
00:20:18,400 --> 00:20:21,840
an octagon groove that's tapered in both directions,

342
00:20:21,841 --> 00:20:25,140
matches the barrel, and the barrel can be finally inserted

343
00:20:25,240 --> 00:20:27,240
into the stock in its full length.

344
00:20:37,480 --> 00:20:40,400
The finest handmade firearms of centuries past

345
00:20:40,500 --> 00:20:44,200
featured elegant details, which Ed replicates using his skills

346
00:20:44,300 --> 00:20:47,740
as a metal worker and etcher.

347
00:20:47,840 --> 00:20:51,340
In the 18th century, rifles needed to patch the bullets,

348
00:20:51,341 --> 00:20:53,340
A cloth patch went around the bullet,

349
00:20:53,440 --> 00:20:56,080
and they were stored in the butt trap.

350
00:20:56,180 --> 00:20:58,880
European style was a sliding wooden patch box.

351
00:20:58,980 --> 00:21:02,180
The American contribution to art in the long rifle

352
00:21:02,280 --> 00:21:03,780
was this brass patch box.

353
00:21:03,880 --> 00:21:07,280
It's engraved and cut out into designs,

354
00:21:07,380 --> 00:21:09,500
has a trap door that springs open,

355
00:21:09,600 --> 00:21:13,200
allows you access to the patch, puts around your bullet.

356
00:21:13,300 --> 00:21:15,900
They snap shut.

357
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:17,840
Each weapon Ed creates not only has

358
00:21:17,940 --> 00:21:21,340
to meet his exacting standards for historical accuracy,

359
00:21:21,341 --> 00:21:24,640
but also for deadly accuracy in the field.

360
00:21:24,740 --> 00:21:26,840
So the ammunition as well as the rifle

361
00:21:26,940 --> 00:21:29,240
is handmade one at a time.

362
00:21:29,340 --> 00:21:32,180
We bring the gun to full cock, and we're ready to fire.

363
00:21:39,600 --> 00:21:43,000
Ed is also a Revolutionary War-era reenactor,

364
00:21:43,100 --> 00:21:45,300
and sometimes he'll make the biggest bang of the day

365
00:21:45,400 --> 00:21:47,700
with one of his rare specialty weapons.

366
00:21:47,800 --> 00:21:50,000
This is an 18th century wall gun.

367
00:21:50,001 --> 00:21:52,900
It's a large fortress-sized weapon used to defend

368
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:56,000
fortresses and to defend artillery in the field.

369
00:21:56,100 --> 00:21:58,500
It shoots this very large quarter pound ball

370
00:21:58,600 --> 00:22:00,000
out to about 500 yards.

371
00:22:07,560 --> 00:22:10,360
Samurai swords are among the most sought after handcrafted

372
00:22:10,460 --> 00:22:11,460
weapons.

373
00:22:11,560 --> 00:22:12,960
The most expensive?

374
00:22:13,060 --> 00:22:19,060
A 13th century blade that sold at auction for $418,000.

375
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:25,000
Although automation has put countless hands out of work

376
00:22:25,100 --> 00:22:29,000
over the past century or so, some jobs are so specialized,

377
00:22:29,100 --> 00:22:32,640
and sometimes so strange, that only highly skilled hands

378
00:22:32,740 --> 00:22:35,840
can accomplish the task. You'll find bone chilling

379
00:22:35,940 --> 00:22:39,260
evidence of this on the outskirts of Oklahoma City,

380
00:22:39,360 --> 00:22:43,760
in the Museum of Osteology. It's owned by a company aptly

381
00:22:43,860 --> 00:22:48,000
named Skulls Unlimited, a leader in processing legally and

382
00:22:48,001 --> 00:22:51,100
and ethically obtained bone specimens.

383
00:22:51,200 --> 00:22:53,700
Its customers range from museums and schools

384
00:22:53,800 --> 00:22:56,140
to the occasional hunter wanting something special

385
00:22:56,240 --> 00:22:58,240
for his trophy room.

386
00:22:58,340 --> 00:23:00,740
Here in the museum, we have over 300 skeletons,

387
00:23:00,840 --> 00:23:04,320
from the white rhinoceros to the humpback whale

388
00:23:04,420 --> 00:23:07,460
to an anaconda that has over 200 vertebrae.

389
00:23:07,560 --> 00:23:08,580
Everything is different.

390
00:23:08,680 --> 00:23:09,760
Everything is independent.

391
00:23:09,860 --> 00:23:12,160
It must be done by hand.

392
00:23:12,260 --> 00:23:14,720
Our articulators, they have to know their anatomy.

393
00:23:14,820 --> 00:23:16,720
Sometimes the skeletons come in as a carcass.

394
00:23:16,721 --> 00:23:19,220
sometimes the skeletons come in as a bag of bones,

395
00:23:19,320 --> 00:23:21,800
so they always have the challenge of sorting the bones

396
00:23:21,900 --> 00:23:24,660
and knowing exactly where each bone goes within the skeleton.

397
00:23:27,660 --> 00:23:31,040
15 pairs of hands currently work at Skulls Unlimited,

398
00:23:31,140 --> 00:23:33,740
seven of which belong to relatives of the owner,

399
00:23:33,840 --> 00:23:35,740
Jay Villamorette.

400
00:23:35,840 --> 00:23:37,400
And there are plenty of new carcasses

401
00:23:37,500 --> 00:23:39,980
to keep them all busy.

402
00:23:40,080 --> 00:23:42,020
What I'm doing here right now is I'm inventorying

403
00:23:42,120 --> 00:23:43,520
a porcupine skeleton.

404
00:23:43,620 --> 00:23:46,720
It's kind of a little bit of a puzzle here.

405
00:23:46,721 --> 00:23:48,120
Being that it has no skull with it,

406
00:23:48,121 --> 00:23:51,460
I'm led to believe that it was a roadkill specimen.

407
00:23:51,461 --> 00:23:54,260
And we just cleaned it up and now inventorying it for sale.

408
00:23:57,160 --> 00:23:58,960
From time to time, we have the opportunity

409
00:23:58,961 --> 00:24:02,560
to work on some very rare endangered species.

410
00:24:02,561 --> 00:24:04,500
Here we have an Andean condor.

411
00:24:04,501 --> 00:24:06,800
This is only one of two we've ever had the opportunity

412
00:24:06,801 --> 00:24:08,400
to work on in 25 years.

413
00:24:08,401 --> 00:24:11,920
It's always a sad day when something so special expires

414
00:24:11,921 --> 00:24:14,220
at a zoo, but it's even a sadder day when something like this

415
00:24:14,221 --> 00:24:16,720
is disposed of and just thrown away.

416
00:24:16,721 --> 00:24:19,880
So we feel what we're doing here is a very important task.

417
00:24:19,881 --> 00:24:22,820
By preserving the specimen, it can go on to teach

418
00:24:22,821 --> 00:24:25,260
for 100 years from now.

419
00:24:25,261 --> 00:24:27,460
One of the first steps in processing a specimen

420
00:24:27,461 --> 00:24:31,220
is the responsibility of Master Flenser, Dale Dorsey.

421
00:24:31,221 --> 00:24:35,080
Flenser's job is to remove as much tissue away

422
00:24:35,081 --> 00:24:38,020
from the specimen as possible with a paring knife.

423
00:24:38,021 --> 00:24:41,480
This is a pronghorn deer that I'm working on

424
00:24:41,481 --> 00:24:44,180
that a customer sent in for a trophy.

425
00:24:44,181 --> 00:24:46,740
I've worked on a large amount of species over the years,

426
00:24:46,840 --> 00:24:51,080
everything from coyotes, bears, badgers,

427
00:24:51,180 --> 00:24:54,520
various types of birds, reptiles, endangered species.

428
00:24:57,360 --> 00:25:00,600
One thing every species has in common is a brain,

429
00:25:00,700 --> 00:25:02,500
at least until it reaches Dale.

430
00:25:02,600 --> 00:25:05,900
After I get all the flesh off, I bring it over here

431
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:08,000
to the brain it.

432
00:25:08,100 --> 00:25:10,800
We use this vacuum device that was designed by our boss

433
00:25:10,900 --> 00:25:14,180
to effectively just suck the brains right on out.

434
00:25:14,181 --> 00:25:16,180
After the skulls are left brainless,

435
00:25:16,280 --> 00:25:18,880
they join other bones on drying racks,

436
00:25:18,980 --> 00:25:21,820
until whatever meat remains becomes the consistency

437
00:25:21,920 --> 00:25:22,980
of beef jerky.

438
00:25:25,460 --> 00:25:28,420
Removing every speck is a job even too exacting

439
00:25:28,520 --> 00:25:30,200
for the human hand.

440
00:25:30,300 --> 00:25:33,440
So the task is left to millions of ravenous coworkers.

441
00:25:36,300 --> 00:25:38,240
After the skulls have been dried for a few days

442
00:25:38,340 --> 00:25:41,240
on our drying racks, they go into our beetle colonies.

443
00:25:41,340 --> 00:25:43,480
This here is a bottlenose dolphin.

444
00:25:43,481 --> 00:25:45,880
It's been drying for about three or four days now.

445
00:25:45,980 --> 00:25:47,180
We'll put it in our colonies.

446
00:25:47,280 --> 00:25:49,180
It'll stay in our colonies about a week.

447
00:25:49,280 --> 00:25:52,880
The durbestid beetles are a carrion, meat-eating beetle.

448
00:25:52,980 --> 00:25:54,320
They consume the tissue.

449
00:25:54,420 --> 00:25:58,020
They get into the crevices that we just can't get into by hand,

450
00:25:58,120 --> 00:26:01,340
and they will literally eat all the tissue from the bone.

451
00:26:07,480 --> 00:26:09,980
After the beetles have finished chowing down,

452
00:26:10,080 --> 00:26:12,440
the bones, like this elephant skull,

453
00:26:12,441 --> 00:26:15,280
are submerged into a hydrogen peroxide solution

454
00:26:15,380 --> 00:26:16,380
to bleach them.

455
00:26:20,380 --> 00:26:23,980
They emerge with that museum-ready whiteness,

456
00:26:24,080 --> 00:26:25,380
like this waterbuck skull.

457
00:26:31,500 --> 00:26:33,600
Now it's time for reconstruction.

458
00:26:33,700 --> 00:26:36,300
And ironically, one of the most difficult things these hand

459
00:26:36,400 --> 00:26:40,600
craftsmen reconstruct is the human hand.

460
00:26:40,601 --> 00:26:42,600
The first thing I'll do is lay every bone out,

461
00:26:42,700 --> 00:26:44,840
make sure that all 27 bones are there.

462
00:26:44,940 --> 00:26:46,940
This one's all here, so the next thing I'll do

463
00:26:47,040 --> 00:26:47,940
is I'll start drilling.

464
00:26:51,540 --> 00:26:58,060
What I'll do is individually drill each bone,

465
00:26:58,160 --> 00:27:03,060
run wire through, and then carefully tighten everything

466
00:27:03,160 --> 00:27:05,500
up and make sure everything's fitting in the correct spot.

467
00:27:07,700 --> 00:27:09,900
The hardest part of fitting this anatomical puzzle

468
00:27:09,901 --> 00:27:13,500
anatomical puzzle together is assembling the wrist bones.

469
00:27:13,501 --> 00:27:16,600
The carpal bones don't fit as snugly as in some mammals

470
00:27:16,601 --> 00:27:17,301
would.

471
00:27:17,302 --> 00:27:19,500
So because we have a lot of motion in our wrists,

472
00:27:19,501 --> 00:27:21,540
they kind of just lay on top of each other and have a lot of

473
00:27:21,541 --> 00:27:24,540
movement to roll back and forth.

474
00:27:24,541 --> 00:27:26,920
Larger reconstruction challenges require more

475
00:27:26,921 --> 00:27:29,120
heavy-duty hardware.

476
00:27:29,121 --> 00:27:31,820
I'm putting the finishing touches on the rear horse

477
00:27:31,821 --> 00:27:32,620
leg here.

478
00:27:32,621 --> 00:27:34,100
This is the femur going on.

479
00:27:34,101 --> 00:27:37,200
It's pre-drilled already so we can get the metal rod through

480
00:27:37,201 --> 00:27:41,200
which basically means it's hollowed out.

481
00:27:41,300 --> 00:27:43,200
That's probably the most easy part,

482
00:27:43,300 --> 00:27:46,200
the bending of the rods, what's difficult.

483
00:27:46,300 --> 00:27:49,240
To hold the bones of articulated skeletons in place,

484
00:27:49,340 --> 00:27:53,040
the crew uses an assortment of wires and steel rods.

485
00:27:53,140 --> 00:27:55,560
The goal of the metal framework is to support and position

486
00:27:55,660 --> 00:27:58,460
the bones in a lifelike pose, while being

487
00:27:58,560 --> 00:28:01,660
as inconspicuous as possible.

488
00:28:01,760 --> 00:28:03,660
The bigger the beast, the sturdier

489
00:28:03,760 --> 00:28:05,960
the metal supports need to be.

490
00:28:05,961 --> 00:28:08,700
This skeleton alone would weigh about 600 pounds,

491
00:28:08,800 --> 00:28:11,360
so it's necessary to build these strong supports,

492
00:28:11,460 --> 00:28:13,260
and they're all done right here in our facility,

493
00:28:13,360 --> 00:28:15,900
and they're built by hand.

494
00:28:16,000 --> 00:28:18,800
The final effect of the meticulous handcraftsmanship

495
00:28:18,900 --> 00:28:21,200
is as eerie as it is fascinating.

496
00:28:21,300 --> 00:28:26,220
Squint, and you can imagine these skeletons springing to life.

497
00:28:26,320 --> 00:28:29,280
In one sense, reconstructing the skeleton of a human

498
00:28:29,380 --> 00:28:32,160
is similar to reconstructing a giraffe's.

499
00:28:32,260 --> 00:28:35,960
Their necks have the same number of bones, seven.

500
00:28:35,961 --> 00:28:39,960
Over the past two decades, computer-generated imagery

501
00:28:40,060 --> 00:28:43,960
has become the standard for Hollywood special effects.

502
00:28:47,000 --> 00:28:48,700
But the handmade variety of effects

503
00:28:48,800 --> 00:28:50,500
can possess a quirky charm.

504
00:28:50,600 --> 00:28:52,720
No software can reproduce.

505
00:28:54,320 --> 00:28:55,420
Show off.

506
00:28:57,620 --> 00:28:59,420
Whose masterful hands are responsible

507
00:28:59,520 --> 00:29:01,020
for these unique images?

508
00:29:01,120 --> 00:29:03,320
It's the Kyoto Brothers.

509
00:29:03,420 --> 00:29:05,120
At their Burbank, California

510
00:29:05,121 --> 00:29:09,360
California studio, Charlie, Steven, and Edward Kyoto have

511
00:29:09,460 --> 00:29:15,760
kept their old school techniques in demand, contributing to films

512
00:29:15,860 --> 00:29:20,660
ranging from dinner with schmucks and elf to the cult

513
00:29:20,760 --> 00:29:23,880
favorite they wrote and directed, killer clowns

514
00:29:23,980 --> 00:29:25,380
from outer space.

515
00:29:25,480 --> 00:29:28,580
At Kyoto Brothers here, we do men in suits.

516
00:29:28,680 --> 00:29:30,380
We do animatronic puppets.

517
00:29:30,480 --> 00:29:34,180
Sometimes we go back to the basics, and we do hand puppets.

518
00:29:34,181 --> 00:29:37,180
My hand is up in this guy, and this is my friend Saddam.

519
00:29:37,181 --> 00:29:40,180
Yes, this is my pre-Spiderhole days.

520
00:29:40,181 --> 00:29:43,180
And we also do marionettes, operated by hand.

521
00:29:43,181 --> 00:29:45,180
The fun thing is, after you build them by hand,

522
00:29:45,181 --> 00:29:47,180
then you get to actually operate them.

523
00:29:47,181 --> 00:29:49,180
Now, this guy, he's manipulated by strings,

524
00:29:49,181 --> 00:29:52,180
so our performance gets translated down through these lines.

525
00:29:52,181 --> 00:29:55,180
And this guy is similar to the puppets we used in

526
00:29:55,181 --> 00:29:57,180
Matt Stone and Trey Parker's Team America.

527
00:29:59,180 --> 00:30:01,180
The other way we bring our characters to life

528
00:30:01,181 --> 00:30:03,180
is through stop-motion animation.

529
00:30:03,181 --> 00:30:05,580
This technique has been around since they invented film,

530
00:30:05,581 --> 00:30:09,120
and it is, in my mind, the most handmade way

531
00:30:09,121 --> 00:30:10,620
you could make a film.

532
00:30:10,621 --> 00:30:13,320
So what the animator does is he moves a puppet

533
00:30:13,321 --> 00:30:16,520
one frame at a time, and then he'll go back

534
00:30:16,521 --> 00:30:18,720
and move the puppet again, take another frame of film.

535
00:30:18,721 --> 00:30:21,860
And when these individual frames are run at 24 frames a second,

536
00:30:21,861 --> 00:30:23,360
you get the simulated live motion.

537
00:30:23,361 --> 00:30:29,200
The Kyoto brothers have been handcrafting films

538
00:30:29,300 --> 00:30:33,600
since they were kids growing up on Long Island in New York.

539
00:30:33,700 --> 00:30:37,000
After honing their skills, they began making their mark in TV

540
00:30:37,100 --> 00:30:39,680
and movies in the 1980s.

541
00:30:39,780 --> 00:30:41,640
But it wasn't long before the advent

542
00:30:41,740 --> 00:30:43,740
of computer-generated effects threatened

543
00:30:43,840 --> 00:30:45,740
to cut their careers short.

544
00:30:45,840 --> 00:30:48,420
When CG came in vogue, you know,

545
00:30:48,520 --> 00:30:50,860
as new people, they were able to make it.

546
00:30:50,861 --> 00:30:53,860
When CG came in vogue, you know, as new things do,

547
00:30:53,960 --> 00:30:55,760
everybody tends to gravitate towards that.

548
00:30:55,860 --> 00:30:59,260
So there was a huge swing in our industry towards CG.

549
00:30:59,360 --> 00:31:00,600
And you couldn't give puppets away.

550
00:31:00,700 --> 00:31:03,300
You couldn't give stop motion away.

551
00:31:03,400 --> 00:31:04,800
But the brothers never lost faith

552
00:31:04,900 --> 00:31:07,720
in their hands-on methods and are proving their value

553
00:31:07,820 --> 00:31:10,320
to a new generation of moviegoers.

554
00:31:10,420 --> 00:31:11,420
There's something about it.

555
00:31:11,520 --> 00:31:12,620
It's intangible.

556
00:31:12,720 --> 00:31:14,520
It's something that the audience is feeling.

557
00:31:14,620 --> 00:31:16,820
There's a connection that they feel with things that are

558
00:31:16,920 --> 00:31:19,020
handcrafted, hand-built.

559
00:31:19,120 --> 00:31:20,160
It is all real.

560
00:31:20,161 --> 00:31:24,160
I mean, no matter how real CG is, it's always fake.

561
00:31:24,260 --> 00:31:25,660
But no matter how fake stop motion might look,

562
00:31:25,760 --> 00:31:26,660
it's always real.

563
00:31:26,760 --> 00:31:28,060
And I think the audiences get that.

564
00:31:28,160 --> 00:31:31,900
Well, it's a living.

565
00:31:32,000 --> 00:31:34,500
It helps that the Kyodos bring a distinctive style

566
00:31:34,600 --> 00:31:36,720
to much of their work.

567
00:31:36,820 --> 00:31:38,020
I wouldn't know how to describe it,

568
00:31:38,120 --> 00:31:40,920
but there's definitely some kind of a Kyoto Brothers quality

569
00:31:41,020 --> 00:31:41,920
to it.

570
00:31:42,020 --> 00:31:44,820
People recognize it, though, and they come to us specifically,

571
00:31:44,920 --> 00:31:46,520
that little Kyoto Brothers edge.

572
00:31:46,521 --> 00:31:50,520
That edge is evident in a project

573
00:31:50,521 --> 00:31:53,160
they recently completed in their spare time,

574
00:31:53,161 --> 00:31:56,160
a gastronomic epic called Innards.

575
00:31:56,161 --> 00:31:59,160
It's a story about U.D. Chunk, a piece of undigested food

576
00:31:59,161 --> 00:32:01,960
who refuses to be absorbed inside the corrupt human body.

577
00:32:01,961 --> 00:32:03,380
And this set is the human body.

578
00:32:03,381 --> 00:32:05,580
It's all made out of pantyhose on wire covered

579
00:32:05,581 --> 00:32:06,980
with rubber latex.

580
00:32:06,981 --> 00:32:09,380
Even the characters are all handmade.

581
00:32:09,381 --> 00:32:11,580
This character, Miller, has been sculpted,

582
00:32:11,581 --> 00:32:14,580
and his mouths are made individually

583
00:32:14,581 --> 00:32:17,080
individually and replaced during the animation process

584
00:32:17,180 --> 00:32:18,680
to simulate the speech.

585
00:32:18,780 --> 00:32:22,080
That was not an illusion, huh?

586
00:32:22,180 --> 00:32:24,820
The Kyoto Brothers are currently working on top secret

587
00:32:24,920 --> 00:32:27,920
projects for The Simpsons and a Japanese theme park

588
00:32:28,020 --> 00:32:29,720
that they can't talk about.

589
00:32:29,820 --> 00:32:33,040
But they did clue us in on yet another work in progress.

590
00:32:33,140 --> 00:32:34,440
When a project comes into Kyoto Brothers,

591
00:32:34,540 --> 00:32:35,840
it comes to the art department.

592
00:32:35,940 --> 00:32:37,040
That's me.

593
00:32:37,140 --> 00:32:40,340
Right now, I'm working on a project with Cassandra Peterson.

594
00:32:40,440 --> 00:32:42,540
We're going to make an animation puppet for Elvira,

595
00:32:42,640 --> 00:32:43,640
Mistress of the Dark.

596
00:32:43,641 --> 00:32:45,280
I'm trained old school.

597
00:32:45,281 --> 00:32:48,920
What I do is I use this advanced tool here, a pencil.

598
00:32:48,921 --> 00:32:50,320
I got some paper.

599
00:32:50,321 --> 00:32:52,120
I'm just working out the shapes here.

600
00:32:52,121 --> 00:32:53,520
It's quick and easy for me.

601
00:32:53,521 --> 00:32:55,320
I've been doing it my whole life.

602
00:32:57,560 --> 00:32:59,280
Once I get Charlie's drawings,

603
00:32:59,281 --> 00:33:01,600
now I start building my sculpting armature.

604
00:33:01,601 --> 00:33:04,000
In this case, I've got this pumpkin that I've got to sculpt.

605
00:33:04,001 --> 00:33:06,520
So I have a tin foil armature here,

606
00:33:06,521 --> 00:33:10,160
and I go into my clay oven that I built by my own hands,

607
00:33:10,161 --> 00:33:12,600
and I then take out this heated clay

608
00:33:12,601 --> 00:33:17,240
and I start laying it up over the armature.

609
00:33:17,340 --> 00:33:18,940
I've been making these kind of sculpts,

610
00:33:19,040 --> 00:33:22,140
or we call them maquettes, for years.

611
00:33:22,240 --> 00:33:23,640
So it's just pretty close to being finished.

612
00:33:23,740 --> 00:33:27,140
Now the next step is to make a mold, cast it out of urethane,

613
00:33:27,240 --> 00:33:28,840
and then it'll be ready to animate as a puppet.

614
00:33:31,660 --> 00:33:35,460
The Kyoto brothers often build their sets by hand as well.

615
00:33:35,560 --> 00:33:39,160
A block of Styrofoam becomes a mountain.

616
00:33:39,161 --> 00:33:42,300
and many details are the results of scavenger hunts.

617
00:33:42,400 --> 00:33:43,900
We go to the wash or to the woods

618
00:33:44,000 --> 00:33:47,500
and find appropriate roots and branches that look like scaled

619
00:33:47,600 --> 00:33:48,900
down large trees.

620
00:33:49,000 --> 00:33:52,100
We find little twigs and branches to accentuate that.

621
00:33:52,200 --> 00:33:55,980
And what looks like grass here is actually fake fur,

622
00:33:56,080 --> 00:33:57,580
painted and stiffened.

623
00:33:57,680 --> 00:33:59,780
What I'm going to do is make it a winter thing.

624
00:33:59,880 --> 00:34:02,520
With the clay, we've made masses of snow,

625
00:34:02,620 --> 00:34:05,160
and I'm going to give it an overall light snow cover.

626
00:34:09,160 --> 00:34:10,660
Damn.

627
00:34:10,760 --> 00:34:14,460
Well, guess I shouldn't have worn heels in the snow.

628
00:34:14,560 --> 00:34:17,040
Combine handmade sets with handmade puppets

629
00:34:17,140 --> 00:34:20,400
move deftly between frames by hand,

630
00:34:20,500 --> 00:34:22,440
and the result is magic.

631
00:34:26,340 --> 00:34:28,680
Oopsie.

632
00:34:28,780 --> 00:34:32,220
In 1925, the Lost World broke new ground

633
00:34:32,320 --> 00:34:33,860
in stop-motion animation.

634
00:34:33,960 --> 00:34:36,660
The scene in which the dinosaurs flee the volcano

635
00:34:36,661 --> 00:34:42,060
the volcano was filmed on a tabletop 150 feet wide and 75

636
00:34:42,160 --> 00:34:43,260
feet long.

637
00:34:47,400 --> 00:34:52,300
Americans spend more than $1 trillion annually on food,

638
00:34:52,400 --> 00:34:56,320
and 90% of that food is processed by machines.

639
00:34:56,420 --> 00:34:58,440
But many are looking for alternatives,

640
00:34:58,540 --> 00:35:02,820
hungry for the human touch, like that of freewheeling

641
00:35:02,821 --> 00:35:07,820
of Brooklyn, New York. His company, Brooklyn Brine,

642
00:35:07,920 --> 00:35:10,760
jars pickles the old-fashioned way.

643
00:35:10,860 --> 00:35:14,160
I started Brooklyn Brine after being laid off as a chef

644
00:35:14,260 --> 00:35:16,660
at a restaurant. Once I was laid off,

645
00:35:16,760 --> 00:35:19,540
I realized I really enjoyed, you know, the process, working

646
00:35:19,640 --> 00:35:22,480
with my hands, making pickles, and just kind of went headfirst

647
00:35:22,580 --> 00:35:24,180
into this.

648
00:35:24,280 --> 00:35:28,620
Sheamus turns out at least 500 jars of pickled produce

649
00:35:28,720 --> 00:35:32,580
on a typical day. His current working

650
00:35:32,581 --> 00:35:35,220
is a restaurant that's only open for dinner.

651
00:35:35,320 --> 00:35:38,980
Every shift is a race against the clock.

652
00:35:39,080 --> 00:35:40,820
Today we're making our whiskey sour pickles.

653
00:35:40,920 --> 00:35:42,320
This is the spice blend.

654
00:35:42,420 --> 00:35:46,120
You start with the mustard seed and the chili flakes for heat.

655
00:35:46,220 --> 00:35:48,500
And the way that heat reacts with your palate,

656
00:35:48,600 --> 00:35:51,300
it opens up your taste buds to different flavors.

657
00:35:51,400 --> 00:35:53,940
The caraway is more of a savory spice

658
00:35:54,040 --> 00:35:55,240
than I'm putting in there.

659
00:35:55,340 --> 00:35:58,840
It's a nice blend between an anisey and cumin flavor.

660
00:35:58,940 --> 00:36:01,880
And the coriander seed is for a lighter kind of herbally

661
00:36:01,881 --> 00:36:07,620
kind of herbally nose. Once the spices are blended,

662
00:36:07,720 --> 00:36:10,380
he lightly heats them. Basically, you're just

663
00:36:10,480 --> 00:36:13,460
toasting them until you can start smelling them, and that's

664
00:36:13,560 --> 00:36:15,960
when the seeds are releasing their oils.

665
00:36:20,940 --> 00:36:27,380
While the carefully chosen cucumbers are being prepared,

666
00:36:27,480 --> 00:36:31,380
Seamus readies the pickling solution.

667
00:36:31,381 --> 00:36:33,580
The secret ingredient for our whiskey sour pickle

668
00:36:33,581 --> 00:36:36,100
is the Finger Lakes Distilling McKenzie Rye Whiskey.

669
00:36:36,460 --> 00:36:41,120
We put the whiskey in first to burn off the alcohol

670
00:36:41,121 --> 00:36:43,860
so people don't get drunk eating these pickles.

671
00:36:44,600 --> 00:36:47,820
The intent of using the whiskey is really for the rye flavor.

672
00:36:49,500 --> 00:36:53,400
Apple cider vinegar, filtered water, and sea salt

673
00:36:53,401 --> 00:36:54,960
round out the brine solution.

674
00:36:58,420 --> 00:37:00,580
Now it's time to hand-pack the jars.

675
00:37:00,581 --> 00:37:03,820
I'm adding three cloves of garlic in each jar,

676
00:37:03,821 --> 00:37:07,020
and Seamus is adding the spice mixture.

677
00:37:10,260 --> 00:37:12,420
Then comes the cucumbers and peppers.

678
00:37:13,120 --> 00:37:15,440
We hand-pack every jar for a couple reasons.

679
00:37:15,780 --> 00:37:17,380
This is another step of quality control,

680
00:37:17,460 --> 00:37:19,740
making sure the freshest and finest Kirby cucumbers

681
00:37:19,741 --> 00:37:20,980
are going into each jar.

682
00:37:21,180 --> 00:37:23,640
Also, we want it to be aesthetically pleasing,

683
00:37:24,040 --> 00:37:25,800
so they're facing the flesh out

684
00:37:25,801 --> 00:37:29,000
and sliding the cayenne peppers in on the side

685
00:37:29,001 --> 00:37:31,780
so people can know that it's going to be a hotter pickle.

686
00:37:33,580 --> 00:37:36,540
After adding the brine solution and capping off each jar,

687
00:37:37,900 --> 00:37:40,540
Seamus and his crew give the pickles a nice hot bath.

688
00:37:40,900 --> 00:37:45,440
Basically, it's creating an internal temperature of 190 degrees,

689
00:37:45,840 --> 00:37:48,060
which does two things.

690
00:37:48,061 --> 00:37:53,400
It kills bacteria, but also it's going to be pushing gases out.

691
00:37:53,960 --> 00:37:57,360
So as the jar cools down, it creates this seal,

692
00:37:57,361 --> 00:38:00,560
and it's in an anaerobic state, a lack of oxygen.

693
00:38:01,840 --> 00:38:04,400
All that's left now is the final packaging work.

694
00:38:05,260 --> 00:38:08,700
It's a very hands-on approach to making pickles,

695
00:38:08,980 --> 00:38:12,400
and I think the benefit is that we're inspecting the jars

696
00:38:12,401 --> 00:38:13,980
one last time before they go out.

697
00:38:14,080 --> 00:38:15,420
It ensures the highest quality.

698
00:38:16,020 --> 00:38:16,880
Orders have been great.

699
00:38:17,020 --> 00:38:18,440
We're moving into a new space,

700
00:38:18,441 --> 00:38:20,200
but right now the restaurant's opening,

701
00:38:20,360 --> 00:38:21,340
and we've got to get out of here.

702
00:38:27,360 --> 00:38:31,060
If you've got a craving for a taste treat that's not sour

703
00:38:31,160 --> 00:38:35,060
but sweet, just blocks away is another standout in the

704
00:38:35,160 --> 00:38:37,000
handcrafted food movement.

705
00:38:37,100 --> 00:38:40,700
My name is Rick Mast, and I'm Michael Mast, and we own Mast

706
00:38:40,800 --> 00:38:42,700
Brothers Chocolate here in Brooklyn, New York.

707
00:38:46,720 --> 00:38:48,020
Let me try the almonds with sea salt.

708
00:38:48,120 --> 00:38:49,120
That looks really good.

709
00:38:49,220 --> 00:38:51,020
Like all good artisan chocolatiers,

710
00:38:51,120 --> 00:38:54,020
Rick and Michael know the secret to making quality

711
00:38:54,120 --> 00:38:57,360
chocolate is the same as for making great coffee.

712
00:38:57,361 --> 00:39:00,360
Start with only the best beans.

713
00:39:00,361 --> 00:39:02,360
These beans come all the way from Madagascar.

714
00:39:02,361 --> 00:39:04,360
They're completely organic.

715
00:39:04,361 --> 00:39:06,360
They're actually all from one farm,

716
00:39:06,361 --> 00:39:10,360
and they come in these big 60 to 70 kilo bags.

717
00:39:12,360 --> 00:39:15,360
The first thing we've got to do is hand sort them for quality.

718
00:39:15,361 --> 00:39:17,360
We go through every single bean.

719
00:39:17,361 --> 00:39:21,360
We strain them through a little strainer we put together,

720
00:39:21,361 --> 00:39:25,360
and then hand pick all of the pieces of huss,

721
00:39:25,361 --> 00:39:29,860
beans that are too small, too flat, pieces of bark.

722
00:39:29,960 --> 00:39:32,200
And then after that, we'll be ready to roast.

723
00:39:32,300 --> 00:39:34,240
These beans from Madagascar are going

724
00:39:34,340 --> 00:39:38,700
to be roasted at about 300 degrees for about 12 minutes.

725
00:39:38,800 --> 00:39:40,740
You know when they're ready is when the whole room smells

726
00:39:40,840 --> 00:39:43,780
like brownies.

727
00:39:43,880 --> 00:39:45,340
So now that we've got roasted beans,

728
00:39:45,440 --> 00:39:48,480
we want to remove the shell from the bean.

729
00:39:48,580 --> 00:39:53,020
So we're going to load these up into our popper, which

730
00:39:53,120 --> 00:39:55,360
first cracks the beans.

731
00:39:55,361 --> 00:39:58,100
After cracking open the beans, this machine

732
00:39:58,200 --> 00:40:01,500
uses cyclonic wind to separate the lighter pieces of shell

733
00:40:01,600 --> 00:40:06,400
from the tasty centers, called the nibs.

734
00:40:06,500 --> 00:40:09,200
Then it's into the grinders.

735
00:40:09,300 --> 00:40:11,300
Because of the high cocoa butter content,

736
00:40:11,400 --> 00:40:14,120
they'll actually just naturally liquify.

737
00:40:14,220 --> 00:40:15,720
The handmade way of making chocolate

738
00:40:15,820 --> 00:40:18,920
is by not adding any emulsifiers, any preservatives,

739
00:40:19,020 --> 00:40:20,220
any extra oils.

740
00:40:20,320 --> 00:40:24,660
So our chocolate is quite simply cocoa nibs and cane

741
00:40:24,661 --> 00:40:27,660
and cane sugar.

742
00:40:27,760 --> 00:40:30,300
Each batch is ground for up to three days

743
00:40:30,400 --> 00:40:34,400
and shelved for about a month to properly age.

744
00:40:34,500 --> 00:40:36,700
Then the chocolate is gently reheated and mixed

745
00:40:36,800 --> 00:40:40,900
to a consistency that can be poured into chocolate bar molds.

746
00:40:41,000 --> 00:40:42,420
Perfect.

747
00:40:42,520 --> 00:40:44,020
Once the bars have been poured, we're

748
00:40:44,120 --> 00:40:46,620
going to go ahead and set them in the refrigerator.

749
00:40:46,720 --> 00:40:48,460
For about 15 to 20 minutes, they'll

750
00:40:48,560 --> 00:40:50,160
sit in here and cool.

751
00:40:50,260 --> 00:40:51,620
Once they're ready, we'll take them out,

752
00:40:51,720 --> 00:40:52,960
and they'll pop right out of those molds

753
00:40:53,060 --> 00:40:54,660
and be ready to be wrapped.

754
00:40:54,661 --> 00:41:00,940
We're currently crafting about 5,000 bars per week,

755
00:41:01,040 --> 00:41:02,160
all in small batches.

756
00:41:02,260 --> 00:41:05,280
Which is a minuscule amount when you think about the industrialists,

757
00:41:05,680 --> 00:41:08,360
which make up the majority of chocolate consumed around the world,

758
00:41:08,800 --> 00:41:10,540
squirting out chocolate by the ton.

759
00:41:10,940 --> 00:41:14,080
This chocolate, handcrafted chocolate, once you taste that,

760
00:41:14,720 --> 00:41:16,780
that's it. You'll never go back.

761
00:41:17,080 --> 00:41:19,120
That's the best chocolate I've ever had.

762
00:41:19,860 --> 00:41:20,740
That's so good.

763
00:41:20,741 --> 00:41:24,740
Hand craftsmanship can not only bring out the ultimate in food,

764
00:41:24,840 --> 00:41:30,980
but also water, the frozen kind.

765
00:41:31,080 --> 00:41:34,980
Just ask Derek Maxfield of Grand Rapids, Michigan.

766
00:41:35,080 --> 00:41:36,680
Derek Maxfield, Grand Rapids, Michigan.

767
00:41:36,780 --> 00:41:38,900
The first time I saw someone carve ice,

768
00:41:39,000 --> 00:41:41,300
I had to get my hands on a piece of ice

769
00:41:41,400 --> 00:41:43,400
and work as much as I could with it.

770
00:41:47,400 --> 00:41:50,740
Armed with power tools and a surgeon's precision,

771
00:41:50,741 --> 00:41:52,540
of the coolest art there is.

772
00:41:52,640 --> 00:41:55,640
I prefer ice just because of the quick removal

773
00:41:55,740 --> 00:41:58,780
and the progress that you can make within a short day

774
00:41:58,880 --> 00:42:00,280
or a few hours.

775
00:42:07,500 --> 00:42:09,500
We've asked Derek to cap off our salute

776
00:42:09,600 --> 00:42:12,800
to handmade wonders with one last creation no machine

777
00:42:12,900 --> 00:42:18,200
could mimic, a final demonstration

778
00:42:18,201 --> 00:42:22,000
demonstration that the hand of man is a modern marvel all its

779
00:42:22,001 --> 00:42:22,701
own.

